Okay, so I'm trying to figure out how to make a website using HTML and CSS. I've heard that HTML is for content and CSS is for styling, but I'm not entirely sure how they work together. Let me start by recalling what I know.

First, HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language. It's used to create the structure of a webpage, like headings, paragraphs, links, images, etc. So, if I want to have text on my site, I use HTML tags for that. For example, <h1> is a heading tag, and <p> is a paragraph tag. That makes sense.

Then there's CSS, which I think stands for Cascading Style Sheets. This is used to style the HTML, making it look better. So, things like colors, fonts, layout, and responsiveness. I remember seeing online that CSS can make websites look good without having to write a million lines of code.

I think the basic structure of a website starts with a doctype, then the html tag with a lang attribute, followed by head and body sections. The head contains meta tags for charset and viewport, a title, and some CSS in a style tag. The body is where the content goes, using various HTML tags.

I'm a bit confused about how CSS is included in the HTML. I know you can have internal CSS, which is in the style tag within the head, or external CSS, which is in a separate file linked via a link tag. Which one should I use? Maybe internal is easier for small sites, but external is better for larger projects because it keeps styles organized.

Let me try to outline the steps I think I need to take:

1. Open a text editor like Notepad or Sublime Text.
2. Start with the basic HTML structure: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html>, <head>, <title>, </title>, <style>, then the CSS inside, </style>, and then the </head>.
3. Add content in the body, using HTML tags like <h1>, <p>, <img>, etc.
4. Write CSS inside the style tag to style the content.
5. Save the file with a .html extension.
6. Open it in a web browser to see the result.

Wait, but I think the doctype isn't necessary for HTML5, but it's good practice to include it. Also, the charset should specify the text encoding, usually UTF-8.

I'm not sure about the viewport meta tag. I think it's important for mobile devices to display the site properly. The content shouldn't be too wide on small screens.

Maybe I should start with a simple example. Let's say I want to create a page with a header, some text, and a button. How would that look?

Something like:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My First Website</title>
    <style>
        body {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 20px;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }
        h1 {
            color: blue;
            text-align: center;
        }
        .button {
            display: inline-block;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            background-color: green;
            color: white;
            text-decoration: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }
        .button:hover {
            background-color: darkgreen;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
    <p>This is my first website created using HTML and CSS. I'm learning how to make a great-looking site!</p>
    <a href="#" class="button">Click Me</a>
</body>
</html>

Hmm, that seems right. The CSS styles the body, heading, and button. The button has a hover effect, which changes its color when you mouse over it. I think I understand the basic structure now.

What if I want the site to be responsive, so it looks good on all devices? I've heard about media queries in CSS. Do I need to add those to adjust styles for different screen sizes?

Yes, media queries allow you to apply different styles based on the screen width. For example, you can have a style for when the screen is wider than 768px and another for screens narrower than that, like tablets and phones.

I should also consider using CSS variables for colors and fonts to make it easier to maintain. Instead of hardcoding colors, I can define them once and change them later.

Another thing is semantic HTML. Using the right tags like <header>, <footer>, <nav>, <section>, etc., improves SEO and accessibility. I think <header> is for the top section, <nav> for navigation links, and <section> for different parts of the content.

I'm a bit unsure about how to link external CSS files. If I have a style.css file, I can link it using <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"> in the head section. That way, I keep all the CSS in one place and it's easier to manage.

What about images? I can use the <img> tag to include images, but I should also add alt text for accessibility and SEO. Also, using CSS, I can style images to have borders, margins, and maybe even make them responsive by setting their width to 100% and having max-width to prevent them from being too large.

